The crash log indicates a "CPU exception on CPU 0" in Machine mode (`mstatus: 00021800`). The `mepc` and `ra` registers point to addresses within the kernel's text section. The `multi_heap` tests, especially when `CONFIG_MULTITHREADING` is enabled, rely on thread-safe memory allocation functions (`k_malloc`, `k_free`, etc.). These functions typically use atomic operations to ensure data consistency across threads.

Upon reviewing the provided Device Tree Source (DTS) file `dts/riscv/aesc/nitrogen.dtsi`, the `cpu0` node defines the RISC-V ISA extensions:
`riscv,isa-extensions = "i", "m", "c", "zicsr", "zifencei";`

The "A" (Atomic) extension, which provides instructions like `amoswap`, `amomax`, `amomin` used in `include/zephyr/arch/riscv/atomic.h`, is missing from this list. If the CPU attempts to execute these atomic instructions without the "A" extension being enabled in the hardware configuration (and declared in the DTS), it will result in an illegal instruction exception, leading to a CPU exception and a fatal error.

The fix is to add the "a" extension to the `riscv,isa-extensions` property in the `cpu0` node of the `dts/riscv/aesc/nitrogen.dtsi` file.

